// The boss journal: the Hydra of the Drowned Temple, Mother of Nine Heads, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).hydra = {
  // the first time this hero meets her (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "She never moves, Sergeant. Sits in the pool, and the heads come round the ring of columns, each to its own turn. I took the hardest head first, because that is the drill. It came back bigger, and the necks beside it lashed out. Look at who stands by a head before you cut it.",
    gloomsworn: "The Mother keeps her watch from the pool, and each head keeps its own from a column, one awake while the others sleep. I cut the cruellest first. Its neighbours woke, and it came back worse at the end. Look at who sleeps beside a head before you wake them.",
    thresher: "She is magnificent: a flooded temple with nine heads rising out of it, every one in a different mood. I took the most savage first, and its neighbours lashed, and it came back stronger for the last stretch. Know who stands beside, and burn every stump. Learned a little late.",
    bloodreaver: "Nine heads, nine different ways to hurt me, and I got every one of them! I went for the meanest neck first, and the two beside it lashed, and it came back bigger at the end. Lesson: look at who stands next to a neck first. The old girl and I are not finished.",
    runeguard: "A mother with nine heads is, in the literal sense, nine sequences at once, and I could not read them. What I missed was the margin: the first head cut returns greater, and its neighbours lash out as it falls. The right cut depends on what stands beside it. Entered in ink.",
    gorgemaw: "Nine mouths, and every one of them tried to feed me something different. Acid, ice, flame, stone. I took the biggest bite first, and its neighbours bit back, and it came back bigger at the end. Check who sits beside a dish before you clear it. Still hungry, but humbled.",
    oathbroker: "The Matriarch holds the drowned temple by right of birth, and each head is a separate party to the claim. The fine print: the first head cut is reissued at the close, enlarged, and its neighbours are bound to retaliate at once. Read who stands beside it before striking. I learned that clause wetly.",
    ironpalm: "She sat in the water and did not move. Her heads spoke for her, one after another, round the ring. I answered the loudest first. Its neighbours shouted back, and it returned, louder, at the end. Look at who stands beside the one you answer.",
    ranger: "Sits in a stagnant pool, never moves. Heads on long necks, each its own weather, each picks a target in turn round the ring of columns. Cut one and the heads beside it lash out. First cut comes back big. Read who stands next to it. Learned it the slow way.",
    shadowblade: "Nine heads and not one of them could agree on where I was, which is how I like to be hunted. I cut the proudest one first, and its neighbours woke, and it came back bigger at the end, with a grudge. Look at who stands beside a head before you wake them.",
    pyromancer: "A Hydra! Nine heads, and one of them breathes FIRE, at me, specifically! I was delighted, and then very busy. I killed the fanciest first, and the heads beside it went off like a string of crackers, and it came back bigger. Check the neighbours first.",
    thermist: "Nine heads, nine elements, every one of them fast, and I loved it! I cut the hottest first and the heads beside it lashed out, and it came back bigger at the end. Result: the first cut returns greater, and it wakes its neighbours. Hypothesis: read the neighbours first.",
    driftblade: "She never moves, and her heads strike round the ring one after another, and I ran in circles between them for hours, never landing. Then I cut the biggest first, and the heads beside it lashed, and it returned bigger at the end. Look at what stands beside it. Tell the hawk.",
    marionettist: "Our leading lady never leaves her pool and keeps a whole chorus of heads to do her acting. I let the company cut the loudest head first; the chorus beside it cried out, and it returns larger for the finale. Mind who stands in the wings. (My Doll took notes.)",
    cleric: "The Mother of Nine Heads, sitting in her still pool like a widow at a wake. I mended after every bite, not before, and ran dry, and one of my flock paid for it. Then I learned that when a head falls the ones beside it lash out, and every stump must be seared.",
    grovekeeper: "Nine heads in a flooded temple, dear, like a hedge nobody has trimmed in a hundred years. I let the biggest bud grow, and when I cut it the branches beside it whipped back. Look at what grows beside a cutting before you make it, and burn every stump.",
    chronomancer: "The Mother of Nine Heads bites in turn, round from the north, like a slow clock striking. I will have mended after every bite; I did, and ran dry. This time I learned that the order we cut them in comes back, and that every cut wakes the heads beside it.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one hydra, nine heads, nine elements, a lab's worth of reagents in one body, and I nearly wept. First cut returns enlarged at the end, and the neighbours of any head cut react at once. Read the shelf before you remove a bottle. Notebook ruined. Worth it.",
    threadweaver: "Nine heads on nine long necks, like nine needles all threaded at once, and not one of them tied off. I cut the sharpest first; the necks beside it snapped, and it came back sharper for the finale. Look at what is threaded beside it, and sear every stump.",
    tidecaller: "She sits in her pool and does not move, and the heads come at us round the ring of columns in turn. Still water gone sour. I cut the cruellest first, and the heads beside it surged, and it came back larger. Look at who lies beside the one you cut.",
    lanternkeeper: "She sits in her pool and does not move, and the heads come at us round the ring of columns in turn. We cut the cruellest first; the heads beside it lashed out, and it came back larger. Look at who stands in its shadow before you cut.",
    kenseier: "She never moves, only sits in the pool, and the heads come round the ring of columns, each on its own beat. I cut the hardest first, and the heads beside it struck on a broken count, and it came back larger. Read who stands beside it.",
    houndmaster: "Great Mother in a stagnant pool, never moves. Heads on long necks pick a target in turn round the columns. Cut the hardest head first and the two beside it lashed out, and it came back bigger. Look who's next to it. Rust nearly drowned learning that.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats her
  win: {
    bastion: "The Mother is down and the pool is still. We held every ring and every column and the line came out whole. I do not say this lightly, Sergeant: that was the best post I have ever stood. Hold the report. I want to sit with them a moment.",
    gloomsworn: "The Mother has closed her eyes, every one of them. The pool lies black and still between the columns, and I sat the last of her watch for her. For once I was not the one who looked most tired.",
    thresher: "The Hydra has gone down under her own water, and the temple may rest. Every stump burned clean, so nothing returns, and nine heads sleep in the dark. I do like a tidy reckoning, and this was a lovely one.",
    bloodreaver: "The old girl sank and stayed sunk. Every head marked me, acid, fire, ice and all, and I wore each scar home. Finest collection I own. Salute, old girl. I am going to miss her, and I am going to find another.",
    runeguard: "The Mother is still, and the account is closed. I read nine sequences and wrote them all down, and not one escaped the margin. Tonight I may close the book with a clear conscience.",
    gorgemaw: "Nine heads and every one of them eaten, or as good as. The pool is still. It was the finest banquet of my life, and the only one that ever fought the whole way down. I am satisfied. Nearly. A little. Ask me tomorrow.",
    oathbroker: "The Matriarch's claim to the temple is extinguished, and every head is struck from the roll. Every debt was met and the final account balanced to the last stump. The contract is complete. Countersigned, with some emotion.",
    ironpalm: "Her heads spoke, one by one, and then all together. We answered each in turn. Then her heart spoke, and we answered last. The water is quiet. I breathed, and it was the best breath of the journey.",
    ranger: "Mother's down. Pool's still. Nine heads, nine lanes, and I read every one. Not a miss in the last stretch. Walked out into the air and didn't look back.",
    shadowblade: "The Mother sank beneath her own water, and the nine eyes closed one after another, and not one of them ever found me. A masterpiece of attention, if I say so. Applause, quietly, from me, in the dark between the necks.",
    pyromancer: "I outlasted the Hydra! Nine heads and a hundred burns, and the last head sank with the whole pool steaming! The one that breathes fire met its match, and every spark I threw is in the song. Worth it.",
    thermist: "She's down! Nine heads, nine elements, every swing in the right direction, and the temple went quiet and I was warm and cold at once and exactly in the middle! Reading: balanced to the last head. Wrote that one on my sleeve, in ink.",
    driftblade: "She sank, and the pool went flat as glass, and I ran out through the water and up the steps and into the open air and kept running, because I could, because nothing was holding me, and the sky was enormous.",
    marionettist: "Curtain on the Mother of Nine Heads. Every stump seared, every head dismissed, the company bowing on dry boards at last. (The Doll took the last bow, dripping. Standing ovation.)",
    cleric: "The Mother is still, and her pool with her. Nine heads bit at us tonight and not one friend stayed in that drowned temple; I counted them out, and counted again. I gave thanks for each, by name.",
    grovekeeper: "She's gone down, dear, and the pool will clear in a season and grow reeds. Nine heads, and my whole lot walked out of her. I haven't been so pleased since the year every bean came up. Sit down. Tea. You've earned the whole pot.",
    chronomancer: "The Hydra fell, and every stump stayed quiet for once. I did not have to undo a single one of us. I will have been proud; I am now, and I expect I shall be again. Someday. Nice to put that down in advance.",
    alembist: "The specimen is dead, regretfully. Nine heads sealed, every sample preserved in my pockets, a few of them leaking. I have never been so happy or so damp. Do not open until I have forgotten what's in them.",
    threadweaver: "She's down, and every thread of mine is still tied to something. Nine heads, and not one of my stitches lost. I'll be sewing up tonight; sit by me and hand me the thread.",
    tidecaller: "The pool lay flat and black between the columns. Not slack water, which still breathes, but a stillness with nothing under it. I sang over it anyway, low, for she was a mother. Something in the deep answered me, a little nearer than before.",
    lanternkeeper: "The pool lay flat and black between the columns, and nothing moved in it. She was a mother, and I took off my hat. Four of us walked out. I left a lantern burning on the temple steps.",
    kenseier: "The Mother is down and the pool is still. Every head had its count, and the last silence was the longest. I sat by the water with the others and said little.",
    houndmaster: "Mother's down and the pool's gone still. Nine heads, nine lanes, and the dogs came out with only a soaking. Ash wouldn't leave the water's edge. Supper came late.",
  },
  moves: {
    "Mother's Cry": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She cried and it reached every one of us, columns and water alike. There was nothing to put the shield to. We came to it already mended, feet set, and took it standing. It came again when she surfaced, and we were ready.",
      gloomsworn: "The Mother cried out, and it reached all of us through stone and water. I made certain the watch was whole before it came. It comes again when she rises. A cry like that has nothing in it but loss. I knew the sound.",
      thresher: "She cries out over the whole temple, and there is nowhere to hide, so come to it full to the brim with mending. It comes round again when she surfaces. A mother does know when to call a family in.",
      bloodreaver: "The Mother howled and it hit every one of us, columns and water alike, and I wanted to answer her! No dodging a cry. We came to it mended. She cries again when she surfaces. Fair. I'd cry too.",
      runeguard: "Her cry reaches all of us, and no column turns it, and no ward. I had the whole company mended before she drew breath; it comes again when she surfaces, and I was ready. There are omens that merely say: be ready.",
      gorgemaw: "A great cry, served to every guest and every column in the room, and no one can eat it for another. We came to the table already mended. It is served again when she surfaces. A mother's cooking: loud, and for everyone.",
      oathbroker: "A flat levy, payable by every party at once, columns or no columns. The sole provision is to arrive whole; it is levied again on her return to the surface. We were whole both times.",
      ironpalm: "She cried out, and it reached all of us. We had no answer but readiness. We stood and took it together. When she surfaced, it came again, and we were ready again.",
      ranger: "Cry. Reaches everyone, no cover. Mended before, not after. Comes again when she surfaces.",
      shadowblade: "She cried out and it went through every column and every shadow, even mine, which I resent. Be whole beforehand. It comes again when she surfaces. A mother shouting is no different from any other.",
      pyromancer: "She screamed, and my ears rang, and every one of us was hit, behind pillars and all! I topped up beforehand and stood my ground, casting. It comes again when she surfaces. A terrible voice, and I say that as a performer.",
      thermist: "Cry! Everyone, columns or no columns. Be whole before it, then cast through it, then shiver. It comes again when she surfaces, so be whole again. My teeth rang for hours. Intellectually, it's fascinating.",
      driftblade: "She cried out and it caught everyone through the columns and the water and I couldn't run round it, so I came to it whole and ran through it, and when she surfaced and did it again I was ready, and ran through that too.",
      marionettist: "The Mother cried out, a long operatic note that filled the whole house, columns and all. The company came to it whole. It comes again when she surfaces. (A rather good soprano, in fact.)",
      cleric: "She cried out, and I felt it in my teeth. It reached every one of us, columns or no. I mended before it came, not after, and again when she surfaced. A mother's grief, and a mother's anger. I know both.",
      grovekeeper: "She cried out like a storm over a marsh, dear, and it reached everything. I had my lot planted with mending beforehand, and again when she surfaced. A cry like that is only weather.",
      chronomancer: "The cry comes a beat after she opens her mouth, and again when she surfaces. I had everyone mended just before it, both times. I was early, which is the correct way to be in a flooded temple.",
      alembist: "Observation: the cry, felt by everyone, no cover; recurs when she surfaces. The big brew goes over the whole party just before, not after. Front shelf. I wrote it twice, to be sure.",
      threadweaver: "Her cry ran along every thread at once, and there was no tying it off. I had my stitches mended before she opened her mouth, not after, and again when she surfaced. A woman should be heard, I suppose.",
      tidecaller: "Her cry ran through the columns and the water alike and reached every one of us. I raised the tide before she opened her mouth, and again as she rose from the dive, for it comes a second time. Nothing to stand behind but a full shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "Her cry went through the columns and reached every one of us, and it comes a second time as she rises from the dive. I let the lanterns burst for the first, and brought every wick back to gold for the second.",
      kenseier: "She cried, and it reached all of us, columns and water alike. No blade turns a cry. We were mended before it came, took it standing, and were ready again when it came as she surfaced.",
      houndmaster: "She cried and it reached all of us, columns and water alike. Nowhere to hide. Mended before it came, dogs close, took it standing. It comes again when she surfaces.",
    } },
    'Rending Maul': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The great jaws came down on me, a wide sweep in front of her. I watched the neck draw back, shield up in the last instant, and the line stood clear of the sweep. A bite like that is just a very large drill.",
      gloomsworn: "The jaws came for whoever holds her front, and that was me. I drew the dark about me and took it whole. The sweep is wide but short. The others stood clear. Even a mother's bite is a wound, and mine are written down.",
      thresher: "Down comes the neck on our knight, a wide sweep in front of her, rather like a scythe with teeth. Keep to her flank and out of the arc. Stray in front and you will be swept off your feet.",
      bloodreaver: "The jaws! Right on me, a great wide sweep, and every tooth in my head rang! A beauty. I braced, took it, and hit her back while it was still fresh. The others kept out of the arc, bless them.",
      runeguard: "The neck draws back and the sweep before her is drawn on the floor; the blow falls on whoever holds her front. I set the ward a breath before, and all others stood to her flanks. Clear in the margin, clear in practice.",
      gorgemaw: "The jaws are the main course, served to me alone, a wide sweep across the front of the table. I opened wide just before it and kept my friends off my plate. Unseasoned, but she means well.",
      oathbroker: "The Maul is payable by whoever holds her front. I accepted delivery behind every ward I carried. Her sweep is wide but only before her, so my co-signatories stood to the flanks.",
      ironpalm: "The jaws came down wide. I met them alone, and took half. The others kept clear.",
      ranger: "Maul. Heavy, on our knight. Wide sweep in front of her. Kept to her flank, out of the arc.",
      shadowblade: "She snapped at whoever stood in front of her, which was never me. I kept to her side, where the jaws do not reach. A mother like that should keep better company than I do.",
      pyromancer: "Jaws as big as a doorway came down on our knight, and I admired the aim! I stayed well off her flank, outside the sweep, and kept casting. A tidy beast; I respect good jaws.",
      thermist: "Maul! Heavy, wide, in front of her. Keep out of the arc. Our knight takes the weight, we strike from the flank, all as it should be. A beautiful division of labour. Now, if only I could take notes while dodging.",
      driftblade: "The great jaws came down in front of her in a wide sweep, and I was already round her flank and through the gap and out the far side, and our knight braced and took the weight of it, and I never stopped.",
      marionettist: "The leading lady's jaws swept the front of the stage and landed on the knight who plays opposite her. The company kept to the flanks. (The Doll applauded from a column. Safely.)",
      cleric: "Those great jaws came down on our knight, as such things always do. I laid the light on him before it landed and asked the rest to keep to her flanks. Dawn keep him, as he keeps us.",
      grovekeeper: "The jaws came down like a rockfall, dear, only in front of her. I'd planted the mending in our knight beforehand, so it was already growing when the stone came down. The rest of my lot kept to the sides.",
      chronomancer: "The jaws fall just after the neck draws back. I had our knight warded in the instant before, which I'm sure I did. The rest of us were already at her flanks, where I've stood since the first time.",
      alembist: "A heavy blow on whoever holds the specimen, and a wide sweep in front of her. A ward on the holder, ready before; everyone else out of the sweep. It clips the curious. I was curious.",
      threadweaver: "The heavy blow came down on our knight, braced and ready, and I ran a little of it down a thread so nobody took all of it. Everyone else keeps out of the sweep in front of her. A neat seam.",
      tidecaller: "The jaws came down on our knight, wide in front of her and nothing behind. I had the water high about him before the bite landed, and the rest of us kept to her flanks, out of the arc.",
      lanternkeeper: "A bite landed on our knight; there was nothing at her back, only the front. I put a lantern on him beforehand. The rest of us stood well to the sides.",
      kenseier: "The great jaws came down on me, a wide sweep before her. I watched the neck draw back, and met them with the blade as they landed. The others stayed clear of the sweep. The neck draws back, then falls: a very clear count.",
      houndmaster: "Great jaws came down on the shield-man, a wide sweep in front of her. Watched the neck draw back and kept to her flank. Dogs behind me, out of the arc.",
    } },
    'Acid Spray': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Acid in a cone, aimed at one of the line and fixed on where they stood when the head began. It does not turn. Sideways the moment it points, and off the burning middle after.",
      gloomsworn: "Acid in a cone at one of us, aimed where he stood when the head began. It does not follow. He stepped sideways the instant it pointed, and we kept off the burning middle. A slow death by acid is the one I didn't get.",
      thresher: "Acid spits out in a cone at one of us, aimed where you stood at the start. It does not follow, so sidestep early, and do stay off the middle afterward, which stays sour a good while.",
      bloodreaver: "A head spat acid at me! A cone of it, aimed where I stood when it started, and it doesn't turn. I stepped out sideways early, laughing, and kept off the middle where it fizzed. Next time I'll take a lick.",
      runeguard: "A cone of acid aimed at one of us, fixed on where they stood at the start of the spitting; it does not turn. Step sideways the moment the head points. The residue in the middle persists; keep off it. A precise creature, in that respect.",
      gorgemaw: "A head spat a hot sour cone at one guest, aimed where he sat when it began. It does not follow the plate. I stepped sideways at once, and kept off the sauce it left in the middle. Too much vinegar.",
      oathbroker: "Acid, in a cone, served on one party and fixed on where they stood at the time of service. It does not amend itself. Step aside at once; the middle remains encumbered afterward. Keep clear.",
      ironpalm: "Acid at one of us, a cone, aimed where I stood. I stepped aside, early. The middle burned after, and I did not go there.",
      ranger: "Acid cone at one of us. Aimed where you stood, doesn't track. Stepped out sideways early. Kept off the middle after.",
      shadowblade: "Acid, aimed where I was standing when the head began. How charming, to aim at where someone was. I was, naturally, elsewhere by the time it landed, and off the burning middle.",
      pyromancer: "A great green cone, spat at me, aimed where I stood when it started, and I moved sideways just in time, though I resented every step! Stay off the middle after, it sizzles. A lovely colour, that acid. Horrid, but lovely.",
      thermist: "Acid! A cone at one of us, aimed where we stood at the start; it doesn't turn. Sideways, at once. Keep off the middle after, it sours the ground. I stood on it once. Singed, sour, wiser.",
      driftblade: "A head spat acid at me, a cone aimed where I'd been standing when it began, and I was already sideways and gone, through the gap and round, and it burned a hole in the place where I used to be.",
      marionettist: "A head spat a cone of acid at one of the company, fixed upon where she stood at the cue. She stepped sideways and the acid met only boards. Keep off the middle after; it eats the stage. (The Doll was unmoved.)",
      cleric: "Acid at one of us, a cone, aimed where she stood when it began. I called to her: sideways, and early. It does not follow. And I told everyone to keep off the middle after, where the acid lay. And they kept clear.",
      grovekeeper: "A head spat acid at one of my lot, dear, a cone of it, aimed where she stood when it began. Step out sideways the moment it points, and keep off the middle after; that ground is spoiled for a good while.",
      chronomancer: "The head points, the acid follows, aimed where I stood when it began. I will have stepped out sideways already. I did, on the off-beat. Keep off the middle after; it burns longer than you think.",
      alembist: "Fine reagent, wasted on floors. A cone of acid at one of us, aimed where they stood when it began; it does not follow. Sideways the moment it points, and keep off the middle while it fizzes.",
      threadweaver: "A head spat acid at one stitch, aimed where she stood when it began. It does not follow, so sideways at once, and off the middle afterward; it stays sour a while. Acid eats thread.",
      tidecaller: "One head spat acid in a cone, fixed on the place its mark had stood when the spit began. It never turned. By then the mark was a long step aside, and the rest of us stayed off the middle, which stayed bitter.",
      lanternkeeper: "One head spat acid in a cone, fixed on the place its mark had stood when the spit began. It never turns. The mark stepped aside early, and the rest of us stayed off the middle, where the acid lay. My lanterns stayed off it too.",
      kenseier: "A cone of acid at one of us, fixed on where they stood as the head began. It does not turn. They stepped aside the moment the head pointed, and stayed off the burning middle after. The head always warns before it spits.",
      houndmaster: "A head swung round and spat at one of us, aimed at where they stood when it began. Won't follow. Stepped aside as soon as it pointed, kept the hounds off the burning middle.",
    } },
    'Tidal Line': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A lane of water drove straight through one of us. Narrow, and long. Whoever stood in it stepped aside, either way. A line is only dangerous to a man who stays in it.",
      gloomsworn: "Water drove straight across the temple through one of us, narrow and long. He stepped off to either side. It is a plain thing, a line. I have been drawn through worse.",
      thresher: "A lane of water runs straight across the temple through one of us, as if drawn with a ruler. Step off it to either side; it is a thin one. Hard to miss, and just as easy to dodge.",
      bloodreaver: "A head drove a long thin lane of water straight through one of us! He stepped off to the side and I wished it had been me. It'd have knocked me flat. A fine little scar. Very straight.",
      runeguard: "A lane of water, straight across the temple through one of us, long and narrow. Step off it to either side. The drawing is clear. The geometry of a line is its own warning.",
      gorgemaw: "A long thin jet of broth, drawn straight across the temple through one guest. Step off it, left or right. It is narrow; it cannot wet more than one at a time. A little thin, honestly.",
      oathbroker: "A narrow lane of water, drawn across the whole temple through one party. It is narrow, so a single step to either side exempts the party. The step was taken.",
      ironpalm: "A line of water crossed the temple through one of us. Narrow, long. He stepped off to one side. It went by.",
      ranger: "Water lane, straight across the temple, through one of us. Stepped off it, either side.",
      shadowblade: "A long thin lane of water cut across the temple through someone, with all the subtlety of a hammer. The unlucky one stepped aside, left or right, which is plenty. I made a note not to be the unlucky one.",
      pyromancer: "A head drove a lane of water straight across the temple at one of us! Water! The insult to a fire mage! I stepped off to the side, sulking, dry, and unextinguished.",
      thermist: "Tide! A lane of water, long and thin, right through one of us. Step off to either side. Don't dawdle. I dawdled once. Damp for the rest of the night, in all directions.",
      driftblade: "A lane of water came straight across the temple through one of us, long and thin, and I just stepped aside, either way, left, right, whichever, and ran on, and it passed behind me like a snapped rope.",
      marionettist: "A lane of water crossed the stage straight through one of the company, narrow and long. A single step to either side clears it. (A rather sharp line, delivered well.)",
      cleric: "A narrow lane of water ran straight across the temple through one of our friends. I called to him: a step to either side, and no more. He did. Water wounds are slow to dry.",
      grovekeeper: "A lane of water ran across the temple through one of my lot, dear, like a stream cutting through a field. Step off to either side. It's thin. A stream can't turn its course for one small stone.",
      chronomancer: "The lane is drawn, then runs, a beat later. Narrow, and long, through one of us. A step to either side. I stepped, and then stepped back by mistake, and wished I had not.",
      alembist: "Note: a lane of water, straight through one of us. Step off to either side. Wet labels peel. Keep the shelf off the lane, too.",
      threadweaver: "A lane of water cut straight across the temple through one stitch, like a ripped seam. Off it to either side, and back together after. Water takes the starch out of a hem.",
      tidecaller: "A narrow lane of water ran clean across the temple through one of us, her water and none of mine. One does not swim against a rip; one steps out of its side. A step either way, and it passed.",
      lanternkeeper: "A narrow lane of water ran straight across the temple through one of us. A step to either side, no more, and it passed. I set my lanterns to the sides of the lane, never across it.",
      kenseier: "Water ran in a narrow lane through one of the others, a long way and a straight one. They stepped out of it to either side. It has no beat to catch; there is only the road, and not being on it.",
      houndmaster: "Water ran in a narrow lane through one of us, a long straight road. Stepped off it, either side. Called the dogs off it as well.",
    } },
    'Venom Bloom': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Poison marked one of the line, more than one man could carry. We closed in on him, all but whoever stood in a ring, and split it. Then out of the bog it left. Shoulder to shoulder; I would call it drill.",
      gloomsworn: "A head marked one of us with poison, too much for one to take. We closed in on him and split it, all but whoever minded a stump's ring. Then out of the bog it left. A dose shared is a dose survived.",
      thresher: "A head marks one of us with poison, more than anyone can carry alone. So we crowd in close and share it, all but whoever keeps a stump's ring, and step out of the bog it leaves.",
      bloodreaver: "Poison on one of us, enough to kill him! We crowded in on him and shared it, all but whoever kept a stump's ring. I was greedy and took the biggest share. Out of the bog afterward. Tasty. Sour. Fine.",
      runeguard: "Poison upon one of us, a dose too great for any single body. All stand close and divide it, save whoever keeps a stump's ring; then leave the bog it makes. The clause is of the most ancient kind: shared, or fatal.",
      gorgemaw: "A great plate of poison on one guest, too much for him alone. We crowded in close and shared it, all but whoever kept a stump's ring. Then out of the bog it left. Bitter, but I took seconds.",
      oathbroker: "A poison debt, too large for a single obligor, laid on one party. All of us close in and divide it, save whoever services a stump's ring. The bog it leaves is vacated after. A shared debt is a payable one.",
      ironpalm: "Poison fell on one of us, too much for one body. We stood in close and shared it, all but the one keeping a stump's ring. Then we left the bog.",
      ranger: "Poison on one of us. Everyone in close, share it. Step out of the bog after. Stump-searer stays put.",
      shadowblade: "Poison on one of us, which seemed unfair on the poor soul. We crowded in and shared it, all but whoever kept a stump's ring, and I resented every inch. Out of the bog afterward. Closeness is overrated.",
      pyromancer: "Poison on one of us! A green cloud that wanted company, so we crowded in close and shared it, all but whoever was minding a stump's ring, and I'm told I coughed with great style. Out of the bog after.",
      thermist: "Venom! Poison on one of us, too much to take alone. Everyone in close, split the dose, except the stump-keeper, then out of the bog the moment it fades. Dilution! The oldest trick! Why does it feel like genius?",
      driftblade: "Poison on one of us, enough to kill them alone, and we all had to run in close and stand still around them, which I hate, and share it, all but whoever kept a stump's ring, and then run straight out of the bog.",
      marionettist: "One of the company was handed a poison cup too large to drink alone. The company crowded in and shared it, all but whoever minded a stump's ring. Then, out of the bog it leaves. (A fine ensemble toast.)",
      cleric: "Poison marked one of us, more than any one could bear. I called everyone in close to share it, all but whoever kept a stump's ring. Then out of the bog it left. A burden shared is a burden borne.",
      grovekeeper: "Poison on one of my lot, dear, too much for one to bear, like too much rain on a single plant. So we gathered in close round the marked one and shared it, except whoever kept a stump's ring. Then out of the bog it leaves.",
      chronomancer: "Poison on one of us, announced a little before it lands. Gather close on the marked one and share it, all but the stump-keeper. Then out of the bog the moment it fades. I will have left the bog already. I left it late.",
      alembist: "Batch notes: poison on one of us, too strong a dose to take alone. Everyone close on the marked one to dilute it, except whoever keeps a stump's ring; then out of the bog it leaves. Diluted, nobody spoiled.",
      threadweaver: "The poison mark wanted sharing, so I drew every thread in close on the marked one and we split it. Then out of the bog, which snags at the ankles. Whoever was minding a ring stayed at it. Tie the poison off in shares.",
      tidecaller: "Poison marked one of us, more than one could carry. We closed in on the marked one, all but whoever stood in a stump's ring, and split it like a catch among boats. Then out of the bog before it dragged. The water was high before it burst.",
      lanternkeeper: "Poison marked one of us, more than one could bear. We closed in on the marked one, all but whoever stood in a stump's ring, and split it among us. Then we left the bog it made. I lit the place where we gathered.",
      kenseier: "Poison settled on one of us, more than one body could carry. All of us but those in a ring closed in on them and divided it, then left the bog it made. Divided, it was a light thing.",
      houndmaster: "Poison on one of us, more than one body can carry. Everyone but those ringed closed in and shared it, dogs too, then left the bog it made. Shared, it's small.",
    } },
    'Flame Gout': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Fire on a pair of the line, a ring following each. Apart, as far as the temple allowed, so neither caught the other's. The rest of us kept off the embers after. Plain work.",
      gloomsworn: "Fire chose two of us and a ring chased each. We parted as far as the temple allowed, and the rest of us stayed off the embers. I have stood in worse ash.",
      thresher: "Fire on two of us, a ring following each, like a pair of lanterns out for a walk. Send them far apart so neither catches the other's, and keep off the embers after. Two lanterns, two corners.",
      bloodreaver: "Fire on a pair of us, a ring following each! I got one, and loved it. We went far apart so neither caught the other's. The rest kept clear of the embers. A fine burn. Another, please.",
      runeguard: "Fire upon a pair of us, a ring following each; the rings must not meet, so the pair go far apart. All others keep off the embers afterward. It is, one might say, the simplest of her sentences: separate, and do not touch the ash.",
      gorgemaw: "A flambe for a pair of guests, a ring following each of them round the table. They sat at opposite ends so neither caught the other's, and the rest kept off the embers. Slightly overdone, as always.",
      oathbroker: "A flame lien on a pair, a ring following each. The pair separate widely, so neither bears the other's debt; the rest avoid the embers after. A case of keeping accounts distinct.",
      ironpalm: "Fire fell on two of us, a ring following each. They went far apart. The embers stayed behind, and we did not go to them.",
      ranger: "Fire on a pair of us. Rings follow. Split far apart, each takes only their own. Keep off the embers.",
      shadowblade: "Fire on a pair of us, rings that followed, how clingy. The pair went far apart so neither caught the other's, and the rest of us kept off the embers. I have never been so glad to be left alone.",
      pyromancer: "Fire on a pair of us, a ring following each! I got one, naturally, and it was the best burning of my life! We parted wide so neither caught the other's, and kept off the embers after.",
      thermist: "Fire! On a pair of us, a ring following each. Go far apart, so neither takes the other's. Keep off the embers after. I was one of the pair, and ran very hot indeed. Delighted. Scorched. Both.",
      driftblade: "Fire on a pair of us, rings following, and we ran in opposite directions as far apart as the temple would allow so neither caught the other's, and the rest kept off the embers after, and nobody burned.",
      marionettist: "Fire on a pair of the company, a ring of flame following each across the stage. They took opposite wings so neither caught the other's. The rest kept off the embers. (Pyrotechnics, excellently timed.)",
      cleric: "Fire fell on two of us, a ring following each. I asked them to go far apart, so neither would catch the other's burning, and to leave the embers be after. Fire and company do not mix.",
      grovekeeper: "Fire on a pair of my lot, dear, a ring following each, like two brush fires. Send them far apart, and leave the ash to cool. Never burn side by side.",
      chronomancer: "The fire lands on two of us, a ring following each, a beat apart. They went far apart before it landed, so neither caught the other's. Embers afterward, to avoid. I was one of the pair. I went far already.",
      alembist: "Fire on a pair of us, a ring following each. The pair go far apart so each takes only their own, then keep off the embers. I once set a bench alight on purpose. Louder, this.",
      threadweaver: "Fire on a pair of stitches, each with a ring that follows. I cut the thread between them and sent them to opposite ends, then kept everyone off the embers. A clean cut keeps the whole from burning.",
      tidecaller: "Fire on two of us, a ring following each. Far apart, as far as the temple allowed, so neither burned in the other's; and everyone off the embers afterwards. Two fires on one deck is a lost ship.",
      lanternkeeper: "Fire fell on two of us, a ring following each. They went as far apart as the temple allowed, so neither burned in the other's, and everyone kept off the embers afterwards. I kept my lanterns off them too.",
      kenseier: "A ring of fire followed each of two of the others. They walked far apart so neither ring met the other, and the rest of us stayed off the embers they left behind.",
      houndmaster: "Fire on a pair of us, a ring following each. Split far apart so each took only their own. Kept off the embers they left. Dogs on the safe side.",
    } },
    'Rime Crown': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Report: the ice ring is safe only at its two ends. Close beneath the jaw, or out past the rim. I put the line at the near end and kept every boot off the middle ground.",
      gloomsworn: "A cold wreath grew about the head, hollow at the throat. I took the near end of it, in the hollow, and the others took the far side, clear of the rim. The ring's middle is where the cold feeds.",
      thresher: "Ice goes out round a head in a wide ring, and the hollow at its throat is quiet. Go in under the chin or stand well out past the rim; the band between is where it bites. Mind the margin, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "A wreath of ice round that head, and the only warm spot was right under its chin! I stood in its shadow, nose to throat, grinning. The thick of the ring is where it bites. I tried it once. Ow! Lovely.",
      runeguard: "A ring of ice about a head, with a quiet hollow at its throat. One stands close in, under the jaw, or well beyond the rim. The band between is the portion that strikes. An exact drawing, and exact in effect.",
      gorgemaw: "A head threw a ring of crushed ice round itself like a collar round a dish, quiet at the centre. I stood right under the jaw, or well out past the rim. Never in the cold band between. Needs salt.",
      oathbroker: "A ring of ice is drawn around a head, with an exempt hollow at its throat. Stand close under the jaw, or well beyond the rim; the band between is liable. We stayed out of it.",
      ironpalm: "The head breathed out a circle. Its heart was stillness. Its edge was cold. I walked into the stillness, under the chin, and the cold went around me.",
      ranger: "Ice ring round the head. Dead centre, under its chin, is quiet. Past the rim is quiet. In between is a bad place to stand. Picked outside; longer shot.",
      shadowblade: "A ring of ice round a head, with a calm spot at its throat. I stood under the jaw, where such heads never look, or out beyond the rim. Never the band between.",
      pyromancer: "A ring of ice around a head, with a calm hollow at its throat! I stood right under the chin, or out past the rim, but never in the cold between. I do not care for being cold, thank you.",
      thermist: "Crown! A ring of ice around a head, quiet at the throat. In under the jaw, or far out past the rim. Never the band between, never the middle of the swing. Edge or centre, my whole life's work.",
      driftblade: "A ring of ice spread round the head with a hollow at the throat, and it's through the hole or out beyond, never along the band, and I ran in under the chin once to see and the band bit me the one time I tried.",
      marionettist: "A frosted ruff round a head, hollow at the throat. The company stands close under the jaw, or well out past the rim. Never the band between. (A fashionable collar. A fatal one.)",
      cleric: "I called to them: either in close beneath its chin, my dears, or far out past the edge of the ice, but never the wide cold band between. Two went the wrong way and I mended them. They will not again.",
      grovekeeper: "Frost threw a ring round itself, dear, thick in the middle and bare at the heart, like a fairy ring in the grass. Stand in the heart, or beyond the edge. The thick of it belongs to the frost.",
      chronomancer: "The ring spreads from the head as it rears. A calm hollow at its throat. In close or well out. I will have stood in the band between at least once, and learned.",
      alembist: "Store at the extremes, like anything that freezes. A ring of ice around a head, hollow at its throat: right under the jaw, or well outside the rim. The band between is where it bites.",
      threadweaver: "Ice round a head, with a quiet hollow at its throat. Close in or well out; the band between is where stitches freeze. I pulled one out of it by the collar. A sloppy seam.",
      tidecaller: "Ice ringed the head, with a hollow quiet at its throat. Safe close in beneath the chin, or well past the rim; the wide cold band between is where the water locks. I stood far out past the edge and sang from there.",
      lanternkeeper: "A head rimed over with ice, quiet at its throat. Close beneath the chin was fine, and so was far outside the rim; the wide cold band between was not. I took my lanterns far out, beyond the edge.",
      kenseier: "The ring of ice left room only at its two ends: right under the jaw, or out past the rim. We took the near end and stayed off the middle. A ring with a quiet end is a courtesy.",
      houndmaster: "Ring of ice round the head. Right under the chin's quiet, and so is out past the rim. Middle's a bad place to be. Took the outside, a longer reach for the knife-work. Dogs followed.",
    } },
    'Stormcall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Lightning fanned from one head in three lanes, the middle through a man of ours. The gaps between were good ground, and so was the far side.",
      gloomsworn: "A head fanned three lanes of lightning, the middle through one of us. The gaps between were safe, and so was the far side. Lightning has an order. It is the only thing in that temple that does.",
      thresher: "A head fans three lanes of lightning out from itself, the middle one through one of us, like the ribs of a fan. The gaps between are safe, and so is well off to the side. Just don't stand in a lane.",
      bloodreaver: "Lightning in three lanes, the middle one coming for me! I stood in a gap, close enough to taste the air, and the others kept wide. A near miss. Best smell there is.",
      runeguard: "Three lanes of lightning fanned from a head, the middle through one of us. The gaps between the lanes are safe, as is the ground well to the side. It is the most easily read of her sequences: three lines, two spaces.",
      gorgemaw: "Skewers of lightning fanned out from a head, three of them, the middle through one guest. The gaps between are good seats, and so is well to the side. A tangy dish. Hard on the cutlery.",
      oathbroker: "Three lanes of lightning, fanned from a head, the central through one party. The gaps between are exempt, as is the ground well to the side. We remained in the gaps.",
      ironpalm: "Three bolts spoke from one head, the middle at one of us. Between them: quiet. I stood in the quiet. The rest of us stood wide.",
      ranger: "Lightning fans from a head. Three lanes. Middle one through one of us. Stood in a gap, or wide of all three.",
      shadowblade: "Three lanes of lightning from a head, the middle one aimed at someone who wasn't me. I stood in a gap, or well to the side, and watched the light go by like a bad idea. Lightning is so vulgar.",
      pyromancer: "Lightning! In three fanned lanes, the middle straight at one of us! Utterly gorgeous and absolutely not mine. I stood in the gap between and applauded through my teeth. A bright, rude rival.",
      thermist: "Storm! Lanes of lightning fanned from a head, the middle one through one of us. The gaps are safe, and so is well to the side. Hair on end. Electrified. Delighted.",
      driftblade: "Lightning fanned out in lanes, the middle straight through one of us, and I ran into a gap between the lanes and stood there with lightning on either side of me, and I never felt so alive.",
      marionettist: "Three bolts of lightning fanned from a head across the stage, the middle through one of the company. The safe places are the gaps between, and well to the sides. (Spectacular lighting, dreadful for the cast.)",
      cleric: "Three lanes of lightning fanned from a head, and the middle at one of us. The gaps between are safe, I told them, and so is well wide. They found the gaps. Bless every one of them.",
      grovekeeper: "Lightning spread out in three lanes, dear, like a storm breaking over three fields, the middle one on one of my lot. Stand between the furrows, or well off to the side.",
      chronomancer: "The lanes fan out as the head rears, the middle through one of us. The gaps between are safe, and so is well to the side. I will have stood in a gap already. I stood in a lane. Once.",
      alembist: "Label: lightning in lanes. Fanned from a head, the middle through one of us. Stand in a gap between lanes, or well to the side. Lightning in a jar is less trouble than it looks.",
      threadweaver: "Lightning in a fan of lanes, the middle one through a stitch. The gaps between are safe, and so is well wide. I kept everyone in the gaps like thread between pins. Never sit on the pin.",
      tidecaller: "A head fanned lightning in three lanes, the middle one through whoever it had picked. Between the lanes lay dry ground, and so did the far side. I kept to a gap in the squall and watched the water on the one chosen.",
      lanternkeeper: "A head fanned three lanes of lightning, the middle one on whoever it chose. The gaps between lanes were dry. I stood in one with my lantern, and kept a light on the chosen.",
      kenseier: "One head fanned lightning out in three lanes, the middle lane running through one of us. The spaces between were free, and so was the far side. I stood in a gap, eyes on the head.",
      houndmaster: "One head fanned lightning in three lanes, the middle one through somebody. Stood in a gap between, or right out wide. Called the hounds in beside me. Never in a lane.",
    } },
    'Stonegaze': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One head stared at everything it could see. The columns were cover: I put the line behind one, stone between us and the head. The ground behind its own column is nearest.",
      gloomsworn: "One head turned its eye on everything it could see. The columns were our cover. Behind its own column was nearest. A man who has been looked at by the dead knows the value of a wall.",
      thresher: "One of them stares over the whole temple like a hard sun at noon, and even my hat is no help. A column's shade will do: put the stone between you and the head, the ground behind its own column being nearest. Anybody caught in the open goes stiff as a statue.",
      bloodreaver: "A head stared at everything it could see! I stood behind a column with stone between us, and felt the glare on the other side. Behind its own column is nearest. I wanted to look back. I did not.",
      runeguard: "A head stares at all that it can see. The columns are the temple's cover: stand with stone between oneself and that head. The column behind the head itself is nearest. I recorded the column numbers; the architecture is an unexpected ally.",
      gorgemaw: "A head stared across the table at everything it could see. The columns were our napkins, so to speak: stone between us and the head. Behind its own column is the closest, and I kept eating.",
      oathbroker: "A head's gaze extends over everything within its line of sight. The columns are the cover provided by the premises: stone between oneself and the head. The nearest is behind its own column. We took shelter there.",
      ironpalm: "It looked at all it could see. I stood behind a column, stone between us. The closest was its own column. I stood there and let it look.",
      ranger: "Gaze. Sees everything in line of sight. Put a column between me and the head. Its own column is nearest.",
      shadowblade: "A head stared at everything it could see, which is a vulgar way to go about it. I stood behind a column, stone between us, in the dark. Behind its own column is nearest. A good place to be unseen.",
      pyromancer: "A head stared over the whole temple like a spotlight, and everything it saw turned to stone! I put a column between us, behind its own, nearest. I like being looked at, but not by that one.",
      thermist: "Gaze! A head looking at everything it can see. A column between us. Its own column is nearest. I ran there too late once and went stiff to the knees. Never again. Stone is a terrible insulator.",
      driftblade: "A head looked out over the whole temple and I ran behind a column with stone between us, the one right behind its own head's, nearest, and I stood in the shade of the stone and felt it look at the other side of me.",
      marionettist: "A head stared at the whole house, a withering look from the leading lady. The company hid behind the columns, stone between us and the stare. Behind her own column is nearest. (A critical glance, and fatal.)",
      cleric: "Whatever that head saw turned to stone. I called everyone behind the columns, stone between us and the head. The nearest was behind its own column. Praise be for columns.",
      grovekeeper: "A head looked out over the temple like a hard sun at noon, dear. I stood in a column's shade, with the stone between us; the ground behind its own column is nearest. Anything in the open turns to rock.",
      chronomancer: "The gaze falls hard on the heels of the head turning, on everything it can see. A column between us. Behind its own column is nearest. I will have been behind a column already. I was behind the wrong one, once.",
      alembist: "Reagent warning: a head's gaze turns to stone whatever it sees. Put a column between yourself and the head; the nearest is the ground behind that head's own column. Shelve behind stone.",
      threadweaver: "A head stared at all it could see. A column between it and my stitches; behind its own column is nearest. One stitch stood in the open and stiffened like starched linen. Never leave a hem unguarded.",
      tidecaller: "One head turned its eye on everything it could see. Stone between us and the head was the only shelter, and the nearest was behind its own column. We crowded into the lee of it like boats behind a mole.",
      lanternkeeper: "One head fixed its eye on all it could see, and nothing but stone could shelter us. The nearest stone was a column. We crowded behind it, the column between us and the head, and I put my lantern in its shadow.",
      kenseier: "One head stared at everything in sight. A stare has no beat for a blade to catch, so we went behind the columns, stone between us and the head. Cover was always close, behind each column.",
      houndmaster: "One head stared at everything it could see. Got a column between us. Cover's close, behind each column. Dogs under the column with me, noses down.",
    } },
    'Mire Spit': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Bog came down on several of the line, a ring on each. We stepped out of our own rings and kept apart, then off the mire it left. There is nothing to plant a boot on in that stuff.",
      gloomsworn: "Bog fell on several of us, a ring on each. We left our rings and kept apart, then off the mire it made. Mud and I are old acquaintances. I never trusted it.",
      thresher: "Bog falls on three of us, a ring on each. Step out of your own, apart from the others, and stay off the mire it leaves, which slows you like wet clay. I do not mind a puddle, but I will not be lingering in that.",
      bloodreaver: "Bog fell on me and two others, a ring on each! I stepped out of mine and kept clear of the others, then off the mire. It slows you like a wound in the leg. I like a slow wound. This was too slow.",
      runeguard: "Bog fell upon several of us, a ring on each. Each stepped out of their own, and kept apart from the others, and then left the mire that remained, which slows. A simple clause, with a heavy footnote.",
      gorgemaw: "Bog fell upon several guests, a ring on each. Each stepped out of their own, apart from the others, and then off the sticky mess it left. Too thick for soup, too thin for stew.",
      oathbroker: "Bog is levied on several parties, a ring on each. Each steps out of their own and stays apart from the others, then quits the mire left behind, which encumbers. Let the record show nobody sank.",
      ironpalm: "Bog fell on several of us. A ring on each. We stepped out of our own rings and stood apart. We did not walk the mire it left.",
      ranger: "Bog rings on three of us. Out of your own, apart from the others. Keep off the mire after.",
      shadowblade: "Bog fell on several of us, which was frankly ill-mannered. Each stepped out of their own ring, apart from the others, and off the mire it left. Mud ruins a good coat.",
      pyromancer: "Bog fell on several of us, a ring on each! Wet, brown, and absolutely ruinous to a good entrance! Each out of our own, apart from the others, and off the mire after. I have been mud-free for exactly as long as I stayed clear.",
      thermist: "Bog! Rings on several of us. Out of your own, apart from the others, then off the mire after, it slows. I stood in it once. Heavy, slow, ashamed.",
      driftblade: "Bog fell on several of us, a ring on each, and I ran out of mine and away from the others and off the mire it left, which slows you, and I will not be slowed, so I never touched it.",
      marionettist: "Bog fell on several of the company, a ring on each. Each stepped out of their own, apart from the others, and off the mire. (The Doll sank to its ankles and did not forgive us.)",
      cleric: "Bog fell on three of us, a ring on each. I asked each to step out of their own and keep apart from the others, and then off the mire it left. It slows. Mind your shoes, all of you.",
      grovekeeper: "Bog spat down on three of my lot, dear, a ring on each. Out of your own, and off the mire it leaves; it slows you like clay. I know my mud. Mud knows me.",
      chronomancer: "Bog falls on several of us, a ring on each, a beat apart. Out of your own, apart from the others, and off the mire after. I will have stepped out already, I did, and into someone else's. Once.",
      alembist: "Bog on several of us, a ring on each. Out of one's own ring, apart from the others, then clear of the mire after; it slows. Label: do not steep.",
      threadweaver: "Bog fell on several stitches, a ring on each. Each out of their own, apart from the others, then off the mire, which holds like wet wool. Tied them back together after.",
      tidecaller: "Bog fell on three of us, a ring on each. Out of our own rings and apart from one another, then off the mire it made. It drags at the ankle like wet sand on the ebb, and I would not have it keep us.",
      lanternkeeper: "Bog fell on three of us, a ring on each. We stepped out of our own rings and apart from one another, then off the mire it left, which drags at the ankle. I lit no lantern on that ground.",
      kenseier: "Several of us were ringed with bog. Each stepped out of their own ring and stayed clear of the others, and none of us stood in the mire after. A soft floor is no friend to a blade.",
      houndmaster: "Bog rings on several of us. Each stepped out of their own and stayed clear of the others, and off the mire after. Soft floor's no friend to a hound.",
    } },
    'Tail Thrash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the jaws, her tail came round in a ring at her feet. I stepped out of it, Sergeant, and back in once it had passed. A drill worth rehearsing: the post is held from the edge of the ring, not the middle.",
      gloomsworn: "Her tail swept the water at her feet, a ring around her body, and I took one stride out of it and came back after. The watch is kept by those who know where the edge is. It is always a little further than it looks.",
      thresher: "A lovely wide sweep of tail round her body, drawn on the water first, like a scythe laid flat. Step out beyond the ring while it turns, then step back to the work. Anyone who lingers at her feet is mown.",
      bloodreaver: "Her tail! A whole ring of it around her feet, and I wanted to take it on the chin, but the drawn circle said no. I stepped clear, let it whistle past, then waded back in swinging. Fair is fair.",
      runeguard: "After the jaws, the tail: a ring round her body, drawn plainly on the water before it turned. I stepped outside the line, waited for the sweep to pass, and stepped back in. A short sequence, easily kept.",
      gorgemaw: "A ring of tail, served around her feet after the jaws, like a second course nobody ordered. Leave the table for it, let it pass, and come back to your plate. Only the ones who hover at her body get a taste.",
      oathbroker: "A second levy follows the jaws: the tail, due in a ring round her body. Those inside the circle at the stroke pay in full. I withdrew beyond the line, let the sum fall on empty water, and returned.",
      ironpalm: "The tail came round her feet in a ring. I stepped out of it, let it pass, and stepped back to her. Nothing more to it.",
      ranger: "Tail. Ring round her feet, after the jaws. Anyone close steps out, waits, steps back. I was never close.",
      shadowblade: "Her tail swept a ring about her body. I was out of it before it moved, which is where I am for most things. Whoever works at her feet steps outside, lets it pass, and returns quietly.",
      pyromancer: "Her tail came around in a great ring at her feet and I cheered, from well outside it! Whoever fights up close, step out when the ring is drawn, then step back in.",
      thermist: "Tail! A ring at her feet, drawn on the water before it turns. Hypothesis: stand outside the ring while it sweeps, then return. Result: confirmed, and the knight does it with remarkable dignity.",
      driftblade: "Her tail swung round her feet in a ring and I was already out of it and running the long way, and then back in once it had gone by, because the circle is only dangerous while it's turning.",
      marionettist: "After the jaws, the tail: a wide ring round the leading lady's feet, painted on the water for all to see. The company stepped out of the light, let it turn, and came back on. (The Doll never left its column.)",
      cleric: "Her tail swept round her feet in a ring, and I begged our knight and the close ones to step out of it while it turned. They did, and came back, and I mended what little there was.",
      grovekeeper: "Her tail came round in a ring at her feet, dear, like a scythe on a long cord. Step out of the ring while it passes, and step back after. I asked my lot to mind it, and they did.",
      chronomancer: "The tail comes after the jaws, in a ring round her feet. I had stepped out before it, being early, and back in after, being earlier. Those at her body should do the same.",
      alembist: "After the jaws, the tail, in a drawn ring about her body. Anyone standing in the circle is struck. Step out, let the reaction pass, step back in. Notes made. My boots are still wet.",
      threadweaver: "Her tail whipped round her body in a ring, and I pulled our knight out of it by a thread, as gently as I could. Step beyond the ring, let it pass, and sew yourself back in after.",
      tidecaller: "A ring went out over the water around her feet and her tail followed it round. I stepped beyond the ring before it turned, as the tide leaves a wall of rock, and came back when it had gone.",
      lanternkeeper: "Her tail swung round in a ring at her feet. I held my lantern high and the close ones stepped beyond the ring, and when it had passed we stepped back to the work.",
      kenseier: "The tail came round after the jaws in a ring at her feet, on its own beat. I stepped out on the count, let it pass, and stepped back. The ring is drawn so there is time to be elsewhere.",
      houndmaster: "Her tail swung round her feet in a ring after the jaws. Called the dogs out of it, waited, called them back. Rust nearly stayed in. Good dog, and lucky.",
    } },
    'Undertow': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A second ring followed the tail, Sergeant, just beyond the first, and I nearly drilled straight into it. The drill is: out for the first, and the moment it passes, back in against her feet. The band between them is a grave.",
      gloomsworn: "The water she threw out came back in a second ring, and the ground between the two was the cruellest place to stand. I waited for the first to fall, then went in under her, or far out beyond. Even the dark keeps a rhythm.",
      thresher: "A second ring chases the tail, a wider swathe dragged back toward her, and the strip between is the part that gets mown. Wait out the first, then step in close again, or keep well clear. A tidy two-step.",
      bloodreaver: "She hit me twice! The tail, and then the water came dragging back behind it in a second ring, and I was standing in it, grinning. Let the first pass, then wade in close to her, laughing. The middle strip is the bruise.",
      runeguard: "The sequence has a second line: after the tail, a wider ring closes a breath behind it, drawn plainly beyond the first. The band between them is struck. Step out for the first; the instant it falls, step inside, or stand far wide.",
      gorgemaw: "The tail was the first course; the second came dragging in behind it, a wider ring of water, and I was sitting in the wrong seat. Wait for the first to land, then shuffle back to her feet. Never dine in the gap.",
      oathbroker: "A second levy follows the first, in a wider ring just beyond it, payable a breath after. The strip between them is the only ground that owes. I stood outside the first, then moved inside it the moment it was settled.",
      ironpalm: "Another ring came behind the tail, wider, closing in. I stood still for the first, then stepped back to her the instant it was gone. The ground between the two rings is the wrong place. I learned it with my ribs.",
      ranger: "Second ring after the tail, wider, a breath behind. Dead ground in the band between. Out for the first, then in tight once it lands, or stay wide of both. I stayed wide.",
      shadowblade: "The water came back after the tail, a second ring hugging the first, and the strip between was exactly where I expected to be hunted, so I was not there. Slip inside her reach once the first falls, or keep wide. Quiet work.",
      pyromancer: "Two rings! The tail, and then the whole pool dragged back at us in a second ring, and the strip in between caught me flat-footed! Wait for the first, then dash in close or stand well back. I did both, one after another.",
      thermist: "Second ring! Wider than the first, a breath behind it, water dragged back toward her. Hypothesis: the strip between is struck. Result: confirmed, wetly. Step out for the first, in the moment it lands, or stay wide of both.",
      driftblade: "The first ring was nothing; the second, dragging in behind it, caught me running the long way round. So now I go out for the first and in under her the instant it passes, because the gap between them is where I used to die.",
      marionettist: "A second ring followed the tail, wider, like a second act that begins before the applause. The company waited out the first and stepped in toward her feet as it fell, or kept to the far wings. The strip between is no place for a curtain call.",
      cleric: "Another ring came dragging in behind the tail, wider, and caught three of us standing in the strip between. I told them to wait out the first, then step in close, or keep wide of both.",
      grovekeeper: "The tail comes round, and then the water drags back behind it in a second wider ring, like a second frost. Stand out for the first, and the moment it passes, step in close, or keep well clear. I asked my lot to mind the strip between.",
      chronomancer: "The second ring comes a breath after the tail, wider, and I had stepped in before it, as I will have done. The strip between the two is the wrong place. Wait for the first to land, then step toward her, or stay wide.",
      alembist: "A second reaction follows the tail, a wider ring of dragged water, and the strip between the two is struck. Out for the first, in toward her as it lands, or clear of both. Boots wetter.",
      threadweaver: "A second ring came behind the tail, wider, dragging, and I found two of us caught in the band between, tied up with nowhere to go. Hold out for the first, then pull in toward her as it lands. Or stay wide of the lot.",
      tidecaller: "The water she threw out came back in, as water does: a second, wider ring, pulling toward her. The strip between the two is where it drowns you. Stand out for the first, step inside the moment it falls, or keep well clear of both.",
      lanternkeeper: "A second ring pulled in behind the tail, wider, and the dark strip between the two caught the ones who lingered. We held out for the first, then walked in close the moment it passed. Or stood wide. Mind the strip.",
      kenseier: "The tail has a second beat: a wider ring dragged in on the count after it. I stood out for the first, stepped in as it fell, and let the second pass over the empty strip behind me. Wait for the count, then move.",
      houndmaster: "Second ring after the tail, wider, dragging in. Dogs out for the first, back in tight the second it landed, or well clear of both. Rust stood in the strip once. Not twice.",
    } },
    'Stump Echo': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "When the second head fell, the stump of the first answered with that head's own attack, from its own place. I stood as I had for the head, but facing the stump. The Heart does the same for all of them, in order.",
      gloomsworn: "A stump remembers. Once a second head had fallen, the first one's stump rose and did what its head used to do, from the same spot. I stood as I would have against the head. The dead do not stay quiet in that temple.",
      thresher: "A cut head is not finished with you, I learned: the stump before answers with its old trick, once, from where it stood. Know what the last head cut could do, and meet it. Tidy, in its way.",
      bloodreaver: "A stump spoke back! I cut the second head and the first one's neck lit up and sent its old attack at us from where it stood. I treated it like the old head, only from the stump. Lovely manners.",
      runeguard: "The margin was clear in the end: a head cut earlier answers from its stump once the next one falls, and it is the same sequence. I read it as the old head, read from the stump's place. A rehearsal for the finale.",
      gorgemaw: "A cut head comes back as leftovers, once. When I took the second, the stump of the first served its old dish from where it stood. I met it as I had the first time, from the new side of the table.",
      oathbroker: "The earlier head's obligation survives its death: when the next is struck, the first stump discharges its old attack once, from its own place. I met it in the same terms as before, relocated to the stump.",
      ironpalm: "I cut a second head, and the first stump answered with its old attack. The same shape, from the stump. I met it as I had met the head.",
      ranger: "Cut a second head: the first stump fires its old move once, from where it stands. Same shape as before. Read it, step off it. Heart does it for all of them.",
      shadowblade: "A cut head still has a shadow. When the next head went down, the stump of the first sent its old attack from where it stood. I read it as the head's own, from its new position, and stood elsewhere.",
      pyromancer: "A stump with opinions! I cut the second head, and the first one's neck answered with its old attack, from where it used to be. I dodged it as before, with enthusiasm. The Heart does that for ALL of them, I'm told.",
      thermist: "Result: cut a head, and its stump repeats the last head's attack once, from the stump. Hypothesis: the Heart is that, for everyone. Confirmed, and I would like it noted I saw it coming.",
      driftblade: "I cut the second head and the stump of the first threw its old attack at us from where it stood, and I was already running, because I knew the shape and now I knew where from.",
      marionettist: "An understudy! When the next head fell, the stump of the one before took the stage and played its old part once, from where it stood. The company knew the lines. (The Doll was taking notes for the finale.)",
      cleric: "The stumps remember. When the second head fell, the first one's stump rose and gave its old attack, once, from its own place. I mended what I could and watched where it came from, for the Heart does it for them all.",
      grovekeeper: "A cut branch sends up one more shoot, dear. When the next head fell, the stump of the one before gave its old attack from where it stood. I moved as for the head, only from the stump.",
      chronomancer: "The past answers: the second head falls and the first stump replays its attack, one beat after, from its own place. I had stood as for the old head, which I'd have been asked to anyway. The Heart is only more of it.",
      alembist: "Remove the second head and the first stump repeats its attack once, from its position. A residue reaction. I met it as the head's own. A preview of the finale, and I wrote that in the margin twice.",
      threadweaver: "A loose end that kept moving: when the second head fell, the first stump sent its old attack from where it stood. I read the shape, knew the head, and stepped off its stitch.",
      tidecaller: "The water remembers. When the second head fell, the first stump rose again with its old attack, once, from where it stood. I met the wave as I had before, only from a different shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "A cut head leaves a dark place, and the dark answers. When the second head fell, the stump of the first sent its old attack from where it stood. I held the light as before, and stepped away from the stump.",
      kenseier: "The first stump answered when the second head fell, with its old attack on a fresh count, from where it stood. I knew the shape and stepped off the line. The Heart is the same count for all of them.",
      houndmaster: "Cut the second head and the first stump barked back with its old attack, once, from where it stood. Same shape. Called the dogs out of it. They remember better than I do.",
    } },
    'Searing': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cut head left a bleeding stump and a blue ring at the foot of its column, and the heads beside it lashed out. I stood in the ring until it closed and it sealed. Miss it and a pair grow back. Mind who stands beside the head.",
      gloomsworn: "Cut a head and the stump bleeds, a blue ring opens at its column, and the heads beside it wake and lash out. Someone must stand in the ring until it closes. I stood several. Nobody thanked the ring. The first cut returns large.",
      thresher: "Cut a head and the stump bleeds, and a blue ring opens at the column behind, and the heads beside it lash out. Someone must stand in it until it closes, or two return in its place. The first cut grows back late, so choose it with care, and hum while you do.",
      bloodreaver: "I took a head off and the stump bled, and a blue ring opened at its column, and the heads beside it went wild! I stood in it till it sealed. Miss it and two sprout. The first cut returns biggest. Pick the one whose neighbours I can stomach. Torture.",
      runeguard: "A head cut; the stump bleeds; a blue ring opens at the foot of its column; the heads beside it lash in answer. Someone must stand within until it closes, else a pair of lesser heads sprout. The first great head cut returns greater: choose it by its neighbours.",
      gorgemaw: "I carved a head and the stump wept, a blue ring opened at the foot of its column, and the plates beside it were thrown at us. Someone had to stand in the ring until it sealed. Miss it and two more heads grow. Mind what sits beside the dish.",
      oathbroker: "A head severed leaves a bleeding stump, a blue ring at the foot of its column, and an immediate retaliation from the heads beside it. One party must stand in the ring until it closes, or two lesser heads are issued. The first great head returns enlarged.",
      ironpalm: "A head fell and its stump bled. A blue ring opened at its column, and the heads beside it lashed out. I stood in the ring until it closed. If no one stands, two heads grow back. Look at the neighbours.",
      ranger: "Head down, stump bleeding, blue ring at the column behind it, and the heads beside it lash out. Somebody stays in the ring till it shuts, or two come back. Check the neighbours before you pick the first cut.",
      shadowblade: "A head fell, a stump wept, a blue ring opened at the foot of its column, and the heads beside it woke angry. Someone stands in the ring till it closes, or two more heads sprout. Not usually me. This time it was. I chose by the neighbours.",
      pyromancer: "I cut off a head and the stump bled, a blue ring opened at its column, and the heads beside it lit up like a row of fireworks! Someone had to stand in the ring until it closed or two more would sprout! It was me. A candle in a cold temple.",
      thermist: "Stump! Bleeding, a blue ring at the column behind it, and the heads beside it lash out in sympathy. Someone stands in the ring until it closes, or two sprout. The first cut returns biggest, so weigh its neighbours. I stood there vibrating. A dull ring, a fine seal.",
      driftblade: "I cut a head and the stump bled and a blue ring opened at its column and the heads beside it lashed out and someone had to stand there until it closed, and I stood, horribly still, because otherwise two more would grow, and now I look at the neighbours first, always.",
      marionettist: "A head dismissed, a blue spotlight opens at the foot of its column, and the heads beside it cry out in sympathy. One player must stand in the light until it closes, or two understudies sprout. The first cut returns larger. (Understudies are the curse of the profession.)",
      cleric: "The stump bled, a blue ring opened at the foot of its column, and the heads beside it lashed out in fright. I stood in the ring until it closed, gladly, for if no one does, two more heads grow. The Mother is merciful in small ways.",
      grovekeeper: "A cut head leaves a bleeding stump, dear, a blue ring opens at the foot of its column, and the heads beside it whip back. Someone stands in it till it closes, or two sprout like bindweed. The first cut grows back biggest, so choose it with care.",
      chronomancer: "A head falls, and the stump bleeds soon after; the heads beside it lash out in the same breath; a blue ring opens at the column behind. Stand in it till it closes, or two more sprout. The first cut returns greatest. Choose it by its neighbours, I will have decided.",
      alembist: "A cut stump bleeds, and the heads beside it react at once. A blue ring opens at the column behind it; stand in the ring till it closes and the stump seals, else two heads sprout. The first cut returns biggest, so check what is shelved beside it.",
      threadweaver: "A cut neck bled, a blue ring opened where the column meets the floor, and the necks beside it snapped like pulled thread. One of my stitches stood in the ring until the seam closed. Leave it open and two heads fray out. Look at what is stitched beside it.",
      tidecaller: "Cut a head and its stump bled, a blue ring opened at the foot of the column, and the heads beside it surged. Someone had to stand in the ring until it closed, or two more heads came up. I let a few swells pass in it myself.",
      lanternkeeper: "Cut a head and the stump bled, a blue ring opened at the foot of the column, and the heads beside it lashed out. Someone had to stand in the ring until it closed, or two more heads grew. I took a turn with a lantern at my feet.",
      kenseier: "Cut a head and it bleeds from the stump, a blue ring opens at the foot of its column, and the heads beside it strike on a broken count. I stood in the ring until the stump sealed. Leave it open and two heads return. I began with the cruellest. Read the neighbours.",
      houndmaster: "Cut a head and the stump bleeds, a blue ring opens at the foot of its column, and the heads beside it lash out. Somebody stays in that ring till it shuts, or two heads come back. Look who's next to the head first, mind.",
    } },
    'Submerge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She dived and the water rose. Slow wading, bad footing. Heads came up at the columns one after another, a ring showing each. The line stayed out of the rings and cut each head before it struck. The order stayed, however slow the wading.",
      gloomsworn: "She dived, and the temple flooded. We waded slow, black water to the knee. Heads rose at the columns one after another, a ring showing each. I kept out of the ring and cut it before it struck. Wading is wading.",
      thresher: "She dives and the temple floods, and we wade through it slow as in a dream. Heads break the surface at the columns one after another, each with a ring first, so step out of the ring and cut the head down before it strikes. Patient work, like the tide.",
      bloodreaver: "She dived, the temple flooded, and we waded like wet bears! Heads burst up at the columns one after another, a ring showing where each would break. I stayed out of the ring and cut it down before it struck. A fun wade.",
      runeguard: "She dives; the temple floods; we wade, slowed. Heads break the surface at the columns, one after another, a ring showing where each will rise. Keep out of the ring, and cut the head before it strikes. Five risings, five rings, five readings.",
      gorgemaw: "She dived, and the temple filled like a stewpot, and we waded through it like dumplings. Heads rose at the columns one after another, a ring showing each. I stayed out of the ring and carved each before it struck. A very wet course.",
      oathbroker: "A submersion of the premises: the temple floods, and all wade at reduced pace. Heads break the surface at the columns in turn, each shown by a ring. Avoid the ring; the head is cut before it strikes. The lease on dry ground is void.",
      ironpalm: "She dived. The temple filled. We waded. Heads rose at the columns, one at a time, a ring showing each. I stayed out of the ring and cut each head before it struck.",
      ranger: "Dive. Temple floods, slow wading. Heads come up at columns, ring shows where. Out of the ring, cut it before it strikes.",
      shadowblade: "She dived, the temple flooded, and I waded in the dark like a stalking cat. Heads rose at the columns, a ring showing where each would break. I stayed out of the ring, cut each head before it struck. Nobody saw me wade, and I rather liked that.",
      pyromancer: "She dived and the temple flooded to my knees, which is absolutely unforgivable for a fire mage! Heads broke the surface at the columns, each with a ring showing first. I stayed out of the ring and cut each before it struck. I steamed. Still do.",
      thermist: "Submerge! The temple floods, we wade slow. Heads break the surface at the columns, a ring showing where. Out of the ring, and cut each before it strikes. Soaked to the bone, and so very alive.",
      driftblade: "She dived and the temple flooded and I couldn't run, I could only wade, which is torture, and heads rose at the columns one after another, a ring showing each, and I waded out of the ring and cut each head before it struck.",
      marionettist: "She dived, the stage flooded, and the company waded through the second act. Heads rose at the columns, each with a ring showing first. Out of the ring, and cut it before it strikes. (The Doll swam, brilliantly.)",
      cleric: "She dived and the water rose around us, and we waded slowly, shivering. Heads rose at the columns one after another, a ring showing each. I called to them: out of the ring, and cut each head before it strikes.",
      grovekeeper: "She dived and the temple flooded like a paddy, dear, and we waded through it slowly. Heads rose at the columns one after another, a ring showing each. Out of the ring, and cut it before it strikes. A patient wade.",
      chronomancer: "She dives, and the water rises a beat later. We wade slow. Heads break the surface at the columns, one after another, a ring showing each a moment before. Out of the ring, and cut each before it strikes. I counted five.",
      alembist: "Spill report: the specimen dives and the temple floods; everyone wades. Heads rising at the columns, a ring showing where each will break. Out of the ring, then cut the head before it strikes. My boots will never be the same.",
      threadweaver: "She dove and the temple flooded, and my stitches waded like wet hems. Heads rose at the columns, each showing a ring first; keep out of it, and cut the head before it strikes. Wet thread knots easily.",
      tidecaller: "She went under and the whole temple filled, thigh-deep and dragging. Heads broke the surface by the columns, one at a time, a ring on the floor before each. We stayed off the ring and cut every head down before it struck. I know water that rises; this rose wrong.",
      lanternkeeper: "She dived, and the temple flooded, thigh-deep and slow. Heads came up at the columns one at a time, each with a ring on the floor first. We kept out of the rings and cut every head before it could strike, wading with a lantern held high.",
      kenseier: "She dived and the water rose, slow and awkward to wade. Heads came up at the columns one after another, a ring showing each. We kept out of the rings and cut each head before it struck.",
      houndmaster: "She dived and the temple flooded, slow wading. Heads came up at the columns with a ring showing where. Kept out of the rings and cut each head before it struck.",
    } },
    "Mother's Heart": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She sealed herself at the end. Each head we had cut struck again from its stump, in the order we cut them, then the living all at once. I had the order by heart, and marched the line clear of each in turn.",
      gloomsworn: "She sealed herself at the end. The heads we had cut struck again from their stumps, in the order we cut them, and then the living ones, all together. I had kept the order. The dead remember who went first.",
      thresher: "She seals up at the end, and every head we cut strikes again from its stump in the order we cut them, then all the living ones together. Remember what you reaped, and when. Each stump answers in its turn, so keep a good count.",
      bloodreaver: "She sealed herself and every head I'd cut came back, striking from its stump in the order we cut them! Then all the living ones together! I kept the order in my head and stepped clear. Terrifying, glorious.",
      runeguard: "At the last she sealed herself. Every head we cut struck again from its stump, in the order we cut them; then every living head together; then her heart. I had kept the order in the margin from the first cut. This is why one keeps margins.",
      gorgemaw: "At the end she sealed herself, and every head I had carved returned to the table, one after another, in the order they were served, and then all the living ones at once. I kept the menu in my head and stepped clear of each course.",
      oathbroker: "At the close she sealed herself, and every head we had cut was recalled in the order of cutting, striking from its stump. Then all the living heads together. The ledger of the cuts was the only guide. I had kept it.",
      ironpalm: "She sealed herself. The heads we had cut spoke again from their stumps, in the order we cut them. Then the living ones, together. I remembered the order and stepped out of each in turn.",
      ranger: "Sealed. Cut heads strike from their stumps in the order we cut them. Then the live ones together. Knew the order, read each stump, stood clear.",
      shadowblade: "She sealed herself, and every head we had cut struck again from its stump, in the order we cut them, then all the living at once. I had kept track of the order, naturally. A good thief remembers what she has taken.",
      pyromancer: "She sealed herself, and every head I'd cut struck again from its stump, in the order I cut them! I kept the order, and then all the living heads together! Most dramatic.",
      thermist: "Heart! She seals up, untouchable. Every cut head strikes again from its stump, in the order we cut them. Memorise the order, move clear at each turn. Then the living ones, together. Home by the pool after.",
      driftblade: "She sealed herself, and every head we'd cut struck again from its stump in the order we'd cut them, one after another, and I ran clear of each in turn, round and through, then all the living at once, and then her heart.",
      marionettist: "At the end she sealed herself, and every head we had dismissed took its encore, from its stump, in the order of dismissal. Then the living heads together. I had kept the running order. (An exhausting curtain call.)",
      cleric: "She sealed herself, and I knew what was coming. Each head we had cut struck again from its stump, in the order we cut them, then the living ones together. I kept the order in my prayers and told everyone where to step.",
      grovekeeper: "She seals up at the end, dear, and every head we pruned strikes again from its stump, in the order we pruned them. Then the living ones together. I kept the order like a planting calendar. Every cut comes round.",
      chronomancer: "She seals, and the cut heads strike again in the order they fell, from their stumps. Then the living ones. Then her heart. I will have kept the order; I did. I rarely get a second pass at a thing that matters.",
      alembist: "Sealed specimen. Every head we cut strikes again from its stump in the order cut, then every living head at once. Keep a written record of the order, read each stump, step clear at each turn. Labels matter.",
      threadweaver: "Sealed, untouchable. Every stump struck again in the very order we cut them, so I kept the order like a pattern and moved my stitches clear of each in turn. Then every living head at once, then her heart. A pattern is a pattern.",
      tidecaller: "She sealed herself, and every head we had cut struck again from its stump in the order we cut them, then the living ones together. I had that order the way a pilot has the reefs, and walked us clear of each in turn.",
      lanternkeeper: "She sealed herself, and every head we had cut struck again from its stump in the order we cut them, then the living ones together. I held the order as I would a row of lamps along a quay, and walked us clear of each.",
      kenseier: "At the end she sealed herself in, and every head we had cut struck again from its stump in the order we cut them, then the living all at once. I kept the order in my head, and we moved clear of each stroke on its beat.",
      houndmaster: "She shut herself away, and each head we'd cut came back from its stump in the order we cut them, then the live ones all together. Held the order in my head and stepped clear of each.",
    } },
    'Regrowth': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the harder ground, a missed ring grew back more heads than before. Nobody leaves a ring early now. I stood each one to the end.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights, a missed searing brings up yet another head. No ring goes unwatched now. I stood them all. The dead keep a good watch on empty places.",
      thresher: "On the hard night a missed searing multiplies the heads, a bill that grows if you leave it on the table. Do burn every stump. I do like to settle up before I go.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard night a missed ring grew even more heads than before. Far too many for my liking, and I like a lot. Never miss a searing. I'd happily take the bites, but not all nine.",
      runeguard: "On the harder road, a ring left empty is punished with a third head, not merely a pair. The margin now carries a bold line: no ring is left unwatched.",
      gorgemaw: "On the harder menu a missed searing serves yet another head on top. Far too many courses even for me. Every stump seared, every time.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, a missed searing accrues interest: yet another head on top of the usual pair. No ring may go unattended. Interest disallowed.",
      ironpalm: "On the harder road, an unanswered stump spoke more than before. Answer every ring. Stay until it closes.",
      ranger: "Hard. Missed searing grows another head on top. Never miss the ring.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard night a missed ring grew still more heads, which is greedy of her. Every stump seared, without exception. A woman who counts her heads should count her rings.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard night a missed ring grew yet another head besides the pair! I stood in every ring after that until it closed, even mid-spell. Especially mid-spell. I sulked, but I stood.",
      thermist: "Hard night! A missed ring grows yet another head. Stand in every ring until it closes, even when you'd rather be swinging. I stood there, vibrating, for the longest ten heartbeats of my career. A dull middle.",
      driftblade: "On the hard night a missed ring grew yet another head besides the pair, so I stopped grumbling about standing still and stood in every ring until it closed, which was the stillest I have ever been.",
      marionettist: "On the hard night an empty ring grew yet another head besides the pair. Nobody misses the ring now; it is the one cue that cannot be dropped. (The understudies are getting out of hand.)",
      cleric: "On the harder night, a stump left unseared sprouted not merely a pair of heads but another besides. I will never let a ring stand empty again. Forgive me that one.",
      grovekeeper: "On the harder night, a stump left unseared came up thicker than ever, more heads than the usual pair. Never leave a ring empty; it is like leaving bindweed in the bed.",
      chronomancer: "On the harder night a missed ring grew the usual pair and another besides. I will have stood in every ring after that. I did; I am not undoing that many heads again.",
      alembist: "Hard-night note: a missed ring grows a pair of heads and another besides. No remedy after the fact. Prevention only: sear every stump. Underlined, heavily, in two colours.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard night a missed ring grew yet another head besides the pair. No stitch of mine leaves a ring before it closes now. Leave one loose and the whole seam unravels.",
      tidecaller: "On the harder nights a stump left unseared put up three heads, not two. So no ring stood empty. Someone was in each from the first moment to the close, the way a mooring is never left unmanned.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights a stump left unseared put up three heads, not two. So no ring stood empty: someone was in each from the first moment until it closed.",
      kenseier: "On the harder ground a ring left open grew back more heads than before. No one left a ring early after that. I stayed in each until it closed.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights a ring left open grows more heads than before. Stayed in each ring till it closed, and nobody left early. Sit, stay.",
    } },
    'Tidal Dance': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Two heads crossed their water lanes over and over, each pass a step further round. The wedges between were never painted. The line shifted to the next one on every crossing. Drill, once you see the count.",
      gloomsworn: "Two heads kept crossing their lanes, each a notch further round. The unpainted wedges between were the only clean ground. I moved the watch from wedge to wedge. The tide is patient. So am I.",
      thresher: "Two heads cross their lanes again and again, turning a notch each pass, a slow waltz round the room. The unpainted wedges are the safe ground, so step into the next one each time. Hum along, no hurry.",
      bloodreaver: "Two heads sweeping lanes across each other, a notch more each time! The gaps between were never marked, so the gaps were home, and I danced from gap to gap like a bull on a pond.",
      runeguard: "Two heads cross their lines of water thrice, each pass a step along. The wedges between the lines are never painted, and therefore are safe. The dance has an order, and I stepped in it. I'm entering the choreography here.",
      gorgemaw: "Two heads crossed their lanes of water again and again, each pass a notch further round, like stirring two soups. The wedges between are never painted. I stepped from wedge to wedge, one dish at a time.",
      oathbroker: "Two parties' lanes of water cross again and again, each a notch along. The unpainted wedges between them are exempt. We moved wedge to wedge, each in turn. Disputes minimal.",
      ironpalm: "Two lines of water, crossing, crossing. Each pass a little further round. The unmarked wedges were still. I stepped from still to still.",
      ranger: "Two heads. Lanes cross, each pass a notch round the compass. Wedges between never painted. Walked from gap to gap.",
      shadowblade: "Two heads crossed their lanes again and again, turning a notch each time, like hands on a clock. The wedges between were never painted. I slipped from wedge to wedge. Always the unmarked ground.",
      pyromancer: "Two heads crossed their lanes of water over and over, each pass a notch round! The unpainted wedges between were the safe ground, so I danced from wedge to wedge, the best choreography of my career.",
      thermist: "Dance! Two heads, lanes crossing, a notch round each pass. The wedges between are never painted. Step into the next one each time. Ballet. I hate ballet. I loved it.",
      driftblade: "Two heads crossed their lanes over and over, a notch further round each time, and I danced it wedge to wedge, through the gaps, and it was the best dance of my life, and I never touched a lane.",
      marionettist: "Two heads crossed their lanes again and again, a step further round with each pass. The unpainted wedges between are the safe places, so the company stepped from wedge to wedge. (A pas de deux, and a dangerous one.)",
      cleric: "Two heads crossed their lanes again and again, a notch further each time. I told them the unpainted wedges are safe, and we stepped from one to the next together, all of us, in step.",
      grovekeeper: "Two heads crossed their lanes again and again, each pass a notch round, like furrows turned crosswise. The wedges between were never painted. My lot stepped from wedge to wedge.",
      chronomancer: "Two heads cross their lanes, again and again, a notch along each time. The wedges between are never painted. I stepped into the next wedge on the beat, three times. A little waltz.",
      alembist: "Two heads cross water lanes, again and again, each a notch further round. The wedge between the lanes is never painted, so step into the next at every crossing. It turns like a stirred pot, and I am the spoon.",
      threadweaver: "A pair of lanes crossing, a step along each time, like cross-stitch. The wedges between are never painted. Wedge to wedge, every stitch together. Keep the pattern even.",
      tidecaller: "Lanes of water from two heads crossed, and crossed again, each time a notch round. The safe ground was the unpainted wedge between them, and it moved with every crossing. We stepped on, wedge by wedge, ahead of the water, and nobody stood still.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two heads laid lanes of water that crossed and crossed again, a notch further round each time. The wedge left unpainted between them was the place to be, and it moved with every crossing. We stepped on from wedge to wedge, and I carried the light after us.",
      kenseier: "Two heads swept lanes of water across each other, again and again, each pass turned a little further. The safe wedges between were never drawn, so I counted instead, and stepped to the next on each crossing. It is a dance, if you know the steps.",
      houndmaster: "Two heads dragged water lanes across one another, each pass a notch further round. Safe wedges between are never painted. Counted the passes and stepped on to the next gap each crossing.",
    } },
    "Hydra's Gaze": { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Every eye she has watched the middle of the temple at once. Only the ground by the outer stones was safe. I had the line against the wall before it ended. Back to the stones, as any sentry knows.",
      gloomsworn: "Every eye she has looked on the middle of the temple at once. Only the ground by the outer wall was safe. I put the watch against the stones before it ended. I know how to stand with my back to a wall.",
      thresher: "On the hard night the whole middle of the temple was watched, like noon with no shade. Only the ground by the wall was spared, so off we went to it, before the look ended.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard night every head watched the whole middle at once. Only the ground by the wall was safe. Back to the wall, before it ended. No scar. Disappointing.",
      runeguard: "Every eye she has turned upon the middle of the temple at once. Only the ground by the outer wall is safe. I had the company at the stones before the look had finished. It was written in advance, and I read it.",
      gorgemaw: "On the harder menu the whole table is watched at once, and only the seats by the wall are spared. Back to the wall, before the look ends. Cold service.",
      oathbroker: "A blanket inspection: the entire middle of the temple, watched at once. The outer stones are exempt, and we took our places there before the inspection closed. The exemption was honoured.",
      ironpalm: "Every eye looked on the middle of the temple. Only the ground by the wall was quiet. We were at the wall before the look ended.",
      ranger: "Hard. Whole middle watched. Ground by the wall's safe. Back to the wall before it ends.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard night the mark watched the whole middle at once, which was unfair even for her. Only the ground by the wall was hidden. My back was to it before she finished. A shadow's favourite place is a wall.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard night every head looked at the whole middle of the temple at once! I adore being watched, but not by nine of them at once! Back to the wall, before it ended, and a bow for the audience of nine.",
      thermist: "Gaze! The whole middle watched at once. Only the ground by the wall is safe. Run for it before the look ends. I ran. I was there first. Wall, not middle. For once.",
      driftblade: "Every head looked at the whole middle of the temple at once and I ran for the wall, through the gap, over the pillars' feet, and had my back to the stones before it ended, and breathed, and felt the look slide off me.",
      marionettist: "On the hard night every head fixed its eye upon the centre of the stage at once. Only the wings, by the wall, were spared. The company exited, backs to the stones, before the look ended. (A dreadful house.)",
      cleric: "On the harder night, every eye turned on the whole middle of the temple at once, and only the ground by the wall was safe. I called them to the stones, backs to them, before it ended. They went at once.",
      grovekeeper: "On the harder night every head looked at the whole middle at once, dear, like a hard sun with no cloud. Only the ground by the wall was spared. Back to the stones, before the look ended.",
      chronomancer: "The gaze fills the whole middle as the heads turn. Only the ground by the wall is spared. I was against the stones already, which is the correct place to have been.",
      alembist: "The whole middle of the temple watched at once. Backs to the wall before it ends; only that ground is safe. The shelves were against the wall all along. Clever shelves.",
      threadweaver: "The whole middle of the temple watched at once. Only by the wall is safe, so I hemmed everyone to the edge, backs to the stones, before it ended. The hem is always the strongest part.",
      tidecaller: "Every eye she had watched the whole middle of the temple at once, and only the ground by the outer wall was safe. I took the party to the stones, backs to them, before it ended, and kept the water high where we gathered.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights every eye turned to the middle of the temple, and the only safe ground was by the outer wall. We went to the stones, backs against them, before it began, and I set a lantern against the wall with us.",
      kenseier: "Every eye she has watched the middle of the temple at once. Only the ground by the outer stones was safe, so we were against the wall before it ended. A stare cannot be parried; it can only be put behind stone.",
      houndmaster: "Every eye she has watched the middle of the temple. Only ground by the outer stones was safe. Got the whole pack to the wall before it ended. Stone at your back.",
    } },
  },
};
